	doi = {10.1016/j.advwatres.2026.105364},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105040203178?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Colloid transport in porous media plays a key role in mass transfer across subsurface environments, yet existing models systematically mispredict particles less dense than water (ρ < 1 g/cm³), including emerging contaminants such as microplastics and remediation techniques using micro-nanobubbles. Traditional colloid filtration theory (CFT)-based correlations, widely applied to dense colloids using simplified Happel sphere-in-cell flow fields, yield unphysical results for buoyant particles. Moreover, the dependence of collector efficiency on colloid density and its potential asymmetry around the fluid density have not yet been quantitatively revealed. To address these limitations, we develop a predictive multi-scale framework that integrates finite element simulations for pore-throat collector efficiency (ηₜ), discrete element methods to extract pore structure parameters, and pore network modeling to upscale and predict macroscopic collector efficiency (η<sub>M</sub>). Two neural network models are trained to predict ηₜ and η<sub>M</sub>, both achieving high accuracy (R² > 0.97). Results show that collector efficiency is strongly density-dependent and often asymmetric. At pore-throat scale, 50.9% of cases exhibit significant density dependence and 55.5% display pronounced asymmetry, while at macroscale, the proportion of cases with significant density effects remains similar, and asymmetry is still notable (35.9%). These findings highlight the interplay among advection, dispersion, and nonlinear directional effective gravity, modulated by colloid size, flow velocity, and pore geometry. A case study on micro-nanobubbles further demonstrates the substantial impact of buoyancy for larger bubbles under low-flow conditions. This framework resolves fundamental predictive gaps and enables accurate modeling of colloid transport across soil, aquatic, and engineered systems. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
