	abstract = {Plant roots play a critical role in regulating surface–belowground hydrological processes, yet the mechanisms by which root regulates runoff, infiltration and erosion under different rainfall events remain insufficiently understood. In this study, root–soil composites of Moso bamboo (Phyllostachys edulis) with three stand densities, including high-density (S1: 2400 plants ha–1), medium-density (S2: 2000 plants ha–1), and low-density (S3: 1600 plants ha–1), were investigated. Controlled rainfall simulations representing low (20 mm h–1), moderate (30 mm h–1), and high (40 mm h–1) rainfall intensities were applied to quantify the dynamic movement processes of surface runoff, subsurface flow, vertical infiltration and surface sediment were collected and quantified using a custom-designed soil water movement monitoring device. Root architecture and soil pore structure were quantified using CT scanning and pore network modelling. Structural equation modelling was further applied to elucidate root–pore–hydrology linkages. Results indicate that (1) under low to moderate rainfall (20–30 mm h–1), subsurface flow was the dominant hydrological component, accounting for 49.87%–88.70% in S1, 50.57%–89.20% in S2, and 41.60%–81.60% in S3 of the total runoff and infiltration amount. In contrast, under high-intensity rainfall (40 mm h–1), surface runoff became dominant, contributing 72.6%–75.0% across all densities, surface runoff rates ranged from 0.052–0.486 L min–1 m–2 across treatments. (2) Relative to S3, under 20–30 mm h–1, surface runoff and sediment yield in S1 and S2 were 11%–32% and 31%–62% lower, respectively, while subsurface flow in the 10–20 cm and 20–40 cm layers increased by 48.31%–54.68% and 27.76%–30.59%, respectively. However, under 40 mm h–1, no significant density effects were detected for runoff partitioning or sediment yield (P＞0.05). (3) Root effects were mainly indirect and mediated by pore structural changes: the total effects of throat radius, connected porosity, and throat length on surface runoff were − 0.29, 0.35, and 0.24, on subsurface flow were 0.43, 0.48, and − 0.41, and on surface sediment were − 0.21, −0.31, and 0.24, respectively. These results suggest the presence of a potential rainfall-intensity threshold, beyond which the regulatory effects of root–pore structure on runoff partitioning and sediment reduction become substantially weaker. Published by Elsevier B.V.},
