	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105040987818?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {In geotechnical systems, the seepage direction often deviates from the gravity direction, potentially altering erosion mechanisms in soil–rock mixtures (SRMs). This study systematically investigates the internal erosion behavior of gap-graded SRMs under varying fine contents (FC = 20%, 40%, 60%, and 80%) and flow–gravity angles (0°, 30°, 60°, and 90°) through an integrated approach combining laboratory experiments and numerical simulations. A novel steerable seepage test device was developed to conduct a series of laboratory erosion tests, enabling precise control of the seepage direction and quantitative measurement of fine particle loss. Complementing the experiments, a coupled pore network model (PNM), computational fluid dynamics (CFD), and discrete element method (DEM) framework was employed to simulate the multiscale erosion process, capturing the evolution of particle migration, contact structure, and porosity under seepage and triaxial loading conditions. Both macro- and microscale responses were analyzed. Results show that fine content (FC) fundamentally governs the erosion susceptibility and structural response. Larger FCs lead to higher fine particle loss rates due to the transition from a stable coarse-dominated skeleton to a weak fine-dominated structure. Concurrently, larger flow–gravity angles reduce the rate and extent of fine particle loss due to increased resistance from gravitational components, especially at higher FCs. The dominant force chain shifts from coarse particle-supported networks at low FCs to fine-dominated structures at high FCs, with greater vulnerability to collapse. Furthermore, inclined seepage induces complex erosion patterns, affecting both the internal structure and global mechanical behavior. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2026.},
