	abstract = {The present study is focused on the numerical simulation of soil media which are consisted of multiphase medium subject to dynamic loading. In many engineering applications, the numerical treatment of soil as a multiphase medium is of a considerable interest. The first attempts to numerically treat multiphase soil media involved applying the finite element method directly by simply simulating the soil skeleton as linear elastic material. Although this method worked well for static cases, in dynamic analysis, the results diverged enormously due to the coupling effects between different phases of soil skeleton and voids filled by water. In this paper, a coupled multiphase model considering both non-linearities of water retention curves and solid state as proposed in the previous work of authors [1]. The solid displacements and the pressures of both water and air phases are unknowns of the proposed model. An important contribution of this paper is that it presents a numerical framework able to consider different constitutive models for simulation of both displacement and pore pressures thus enabling simulation of different problems in semi and fully saturated soil media. The computational aspects of the coupled finite elements present both linear and non-linear material laws for porous media. In numerical integration of the non-linear material models the error controlling is improved by additional loop which improve the simulation correctness. In this work, the mechanical behavior of the solid state of multiphase material and the interaction among the phases are considered through mass conservation and momentum balance equations. The equilibrium equations include the soil skeleton’s constitutive behavior. Pressures of water and air phases and displacements in both horizontal and vertical directions are taken as independent variables and in the same time present the degrees of freedom. In partially saturated state of the soil medium the voids are filled with partially by water in which the total saturation equals the sum of air and water saturation percentages which result in unity. Consequently, the stress terms consider the effective stress as stress on the solid state and the pressures in the pore space of the soil medium. The dissipative terms arising in the multiphase model at the interfaces are considered by relative permeability. In order to derive the weak form of governing equations the Galerkin’s weighted residual method. The developed new model for simulation of dynamic problems in soil media is implemented in the finite element software ANSYS by using its user programmable features which allow programming of different material models. The proposed method is demonstrated through simulation of seepage tests, partially consolidation problems and triaxial experiments. In all simulations the advantages of using the new model are discussed and compared accordingly. © 2021, International Association for Earthquake Engineering. All rights reserved.},
