	pages = {620 - 625},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84885698846?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Water characteristics of the unsaturated soils are usually determined utilizing the relationship between the suction value and water content of the soil (soil water retention curve). This curve has various branches based on the suction imposition path. Knowing the structure of the porous material (pore size distribution) paves the way for determining all branches of the soil water retention curve. But, experimental determination of the pore size distribution of the soils is costly and time consuming. In this paper, a new percolation approach is presented to determine the pore size distribution of the soil using the boundary drying curve of the soil water retention. This new method covers a wide range of suction values (0-15000kPa); it also can incorporate as many trials required, to increase the probability of reaching to an acceptable answer (pore size distribution), and fi nally, different sizes of pore network (D×D×D) can be analyzed to reach at the optimum size for the answer. The artifi cial pore network model is constructed through an iteration procedure to minimize the error between the experimentally determined drying boundary of the soil water retention curve and the drying curve predicted by the pore network. The developed pore network is then utilized to simulate the wetting branch of the soil water retention curve. Besides, mechanical loading of the soils leads to a change in the pore size distribution and consequently, the soil water retention curves. In this study, pore size distribution curves at different net confi ning stresses are modeled and the effect of stress state on the pores are discussed.},
	author_keywords = {Percolation theory; Pore network; Soil water retention curve},
	keywords = {Drying; Geomechanics; Iterative methods; Pore size; Porous materials; Solvents; Effect of stress state; Experimental determination; Hysteresis phenomenon; Percolation theory; Pore networks; Pore-network modeling; Soil water retention curves; Water characteristics; Soil moisture},
