	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/58849133897?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {We demonstrate and validate predictive pore-scale modeling: we start with three-dimensional images of small rock samples obtained using micro-CT scanning with a resolution of a few microns, extract networks from these images and then predict multiphase flow properties by simulating capillary-controlled displacement. We study two sand packs, a poorly consolidated sandstone, Berea sandstone and a carbonate. Single-phase flow properties can be computed on a binarized image directly: we calculate the absolute permeability, resistivity and NMR response. We also extract topologically equivalent networks of pores and throats using a maximal ball method. As a quality control we compare single-phase predictions on these networks with those obtained on the images and from experiment: the permeability and NMR response are similar although we tend to underestimate the resistivity. Networks representing consolidated media tend to over-estimate the magnetization decay in an NMR experiment. We then compute multiphase properties, including capillary pressure, relative permeability and NMR response as a function of wettability (the contact angle distribution assigned to pores and throats). Experimental data, where available, is used to validate our predictions; where we know the wettability and pore structure, we are able to predict multiphase flow properties accurately. We show how relative permeability and capillary pressure is affected by rock type - principally the coordination number of the pores and the pore size distribution - and wettability. We suggest that predictive pore-scale modeling combined with micro-CT imaging is a useful tool, complementary to special core analysis, for the determination of single and multiphase flow properties. Copyright 2008, Society of Petroleum Engineers.},
	keywords = {Capillarity; Capillary flow; Capillary tubes; Computerized tomography; Contact angle; Customer satisfaction; Electric instrument transformers; Fluid dynamics; Forecasting; Imaging systems; Information retrieval systems; Liquids; Multiphase flow; Nuclear magnetic resonance; Nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy; Pore size; Quality assurance; Quality control; Quality function deployment; Sandstone; Soil structure interactions; Solar radiation; Solids; Three dimensional; Total quality management; Absolute permeabilities; Angle distributions; Berea sandstones; Capillary pressures; Co-ordination numbers; Ct imaging; CT-scanning; Equivalent networks; Experimental datum; Magnetization decays; Multi-phase flow properties; Multi-phase flow simulations; Phase predictions; Pore size distributions; Pore-scale modeling; Relative permeabilities; Rock samples; Rock types; Sand packs; Single-phase flows; Special core analysis; Three-dimensional images; Image processing},
